The  program  is used to manage Bluetooth Link Key storage.  Keys
are normally handled by the daemon which caches them in the  file
and  provides them as required when Bluetooth connections need to
be authenticated.  These keys are required  for  connections  be‐
tween  remote  devices  and  the  specific  controller and so for
multi‐boot systems where it may not always be possible to specify
the same key across all OS’s it can be better to have  the  Blue‐
tooth  controller  provide the keys directly from its semi‐perma‐
nent memory once devices are paired.  will read, write  or  clear
keys  in  device  memory or the key cache as required.  Note that
without the daemon running users will be unable to  supply  PINs,
and Link Keys resulting from new pairings will not be stored.  If
no  new pairings are expected and the keys are stored in the con‐
troller then is not required.  The options are as follows:  Spec‐
ify  the  remote  device  address.  May be given as a BDADDR or a
name.  Clear key from device.  Clear key from file.  Specify  the
local  device address.  May be given as a BDADDR or a name.  Sup‐
ply a Link Key as a string of hexadecimal digits.  Up to 32  dig‐
its  will  be processed and the resulting key will be zero padded
to 16 octets.  List keys stored in device.  List keys  stored  in
file.   Read  key from device.  Read key from file.  Write key to
device.  Write key to file.  Super‐user privileges  are  required
to read or write link keys.  Read key for mouse at ubt0 from file
and  write  to  device Write new key for keyboard at ubt0 to file
Clear key for phone at bt3c0 from file and device
